# App lifecycle & background execution

**Primary source:** the Activity/Fragment lifecycle, `ProcessLifecycleOwner`
for app-wide foreground/background transitions, and WorkManager for
deferrable/guaranteed background work — all Jetpack/AOSP-native. No
third-party layer is needed here.

## App lifecycle

- Observe `ProcessLifecycleOwner.get().lifecycle` for app-wide
  foreground/background transitions (analogous to iOS's `ScenePhase`)
  instead of tracking `onResume`/`onPause` across every Activity
  individually.
- **On moving to background:** persist any in-flight user input (draft
  state) via the `core/persistence` layer, and stop any polling/timers
  that don't need to run off-screen.
- **On returning to foreground:** re-validate the session token is not
  expired (`security-baseline-mobile.mdc`) before resuming a screen that
  shows authenticated data — do not assume a token is still valid just
  because the process didn't fully die.
- Handle **process death** explicitly: `ViewModel`'s `SavedStateHandle`
  restores simple UI state (selected tab, form field values); anything
  more (session validity, cached data) is re-derived from
  `core/persistence`/`AuthRepository` on recreation, not assumed to
  survive.

## `ViewModel` scoping

- `ViewModel`s are scoped to the Activity/Navigation-destination that
  owns the screen — they survive configuration changes automatically
  but are cleared on navigation away, per `architecture-udf-state.mdc`.
- Do not hold a `ViewModel` reference longer than its natural scope (e.g.
  in a singleton) to "cache" its state — use `core/persistence` or a
  repository for state that should outlive a screen.

## Background execution (WorkManager)

- Register deferrable/guaranteed background work (periodic sync, retry
  of a failed upload) as a `WorkRequest` via WorkManager — not a
  `Service` with a manually managed lifecycle, and not a bare coroutine
  launched from an Activity that will be destroyed.
- Every `WorkRequest` declares explicit `Constraints` (network required,
  battery-not-low, etc.) so the system can defer work appropriately
  under Doze/App Standby rather than the app fighting the OS's power
  management.
- Background work goes through the same `core` services
  (`ApiClient`, `AppDatabase`) as foreground work via Hilt's
  `HiltWorkerFactory` — no separate background-only networking path.
- Use `enqueueUniqueWork`/`enqueueUniquePeriodicWork` with an explicit
  policy (`KEEP`/`REPLACE`) for anything that must not be scheduled twice
  — e.g. triggering a sync `WorkRequest` from an FCM data message
  (`push-notifications.mdc`).

## Anti-patterns

- **A long-running foreground `Service` used as a substitute for
  WorkManager** for work that isn't actually user-visible/immediate.
- **Assuming background time is unlimited** — always design for
  Doze/App Standby deferring work, and for the process being killed
  entirely.
- **Storing tokens or PII in `SavedStateHandle`** — see
  `security-baseline-mobile.mdc`.

## Related rules

- **Security/session validity:** `security-baseline-mobile.mdc`
- **Persistence:** `persistence-room.mdc`
- **Push (triggering WorkManager from FCM):** `push-notifications.mdc`
